  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 144 AM EDT Wed Apr 22 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge will move across the eastern CONUS Thursday \nnight into Friday while surface high pressure centered to the \nsoutheast of Georgia gradually weakens. Warm and dry conditions \nare expected to persist under the ridge through Friday in spite of\nsouthwesterly low level flow on the back side of the high. \nOvernight lows are expected to range from the mid 50s to near 60. \nHigh temperatures will be about 8-12 degrees above daily normals \nacross the forecast area, rising into the mid to upper 80s (and \nupper 70s in the far northeastern mountains). With strong diurnal \nmixing, relative humidity values are forecast to drop to between \n25-35% on Friday afternoon. With very dry fuels and ongoing \ndrought conditions, a Fire Danger Statement will likely be needed \nfor at least a portion of the area once again.\n\nA large-scale change in the upper level flow pattern will begin \non Friday night into Saturday morning. As the axis of the ridge \nclears Georgia to the east, troughing extending from a closed low \nover the northern High Plains will swing through the Mississippi \nRiver Valley region. As this trough rotates northeastward, it will\ndrive a surface low through the upper Midwest, which will in turn\nadvance a cold front towards north Georgia. Showers and \nthunderstorms ahead of this front are anticipated to enter far \nnorthwest Georgia late Friday night, advancing south and east over\nthe course of the day on Saturday as the front slowly pushes \nthrough the area. Low level shear and forcing ahead of the front \nare progged to be fairly weak at this time, limiting the potential\nfor widespread severe weather. However, with increasing dewpoints\nin the afternoon and temperatures rising into the upper 70s along\nand north of the I-85 corridor and 80s to the south, instability \nmay become sufficient to support isolated strong storms where \nconditions align. Such storms will be capable of producing gusty \nwinds and frequent lightning.\n\nAn unsettled pattern will persist on Sunday into next week as the\nfront stalls across south/central Georgia. Chance PoPs will \npersist on Sunday into Monday as weak mid-level disturbances \noverrun this boundary. Late Monday into Tuesday, a shortwave \ntrough and strengthening low pressure system will advance from the\nGreat Plains through the Midwest, with another cold front \nextending through the southeast. More widespread showers and \nthunderstorms are forecast to return late Monday night into early \nTuesday, with chances diminishing as the front exits the forecast \narea to the southeast Tuesday night. With a warm and moist \nenvironment in place ahead of the front and more robust shear \nprofiles compared to Saturday, the potential for severe weather \nassociated with this system will need to be monitored. Rainfall \namounts from Friday night through Tuesday are forecast to range \nfrom about 0.5\" in east-central Georgia to 2\" in far northwest \nGeorgia, though locally higher amounts will be possible with \nstronger storms that occur.",
